Apple trackpads: macOS's resting finger, and the firmware's palms instead of strips.

How macOS 27.2 keeps hands at rest out of the way, from HSTouchHIDService
(the user-space gesture engine of the built-in trackpad):

- Palms and edge contacts are the trackpad firmware's verdict, not the
  host's. Every contact record carries a finger identity (byte 2): 1 thumb,
  2-5 fingers, 6-11 palm, 12-14 edge contact, 15 ignored, 0 unidentified;
  the engine counts only 1-5 as fingers (MTParserPath_::isFingerContact and
  friends, 0x6ffe4-0x70200), and nothing else moves the pointer, takes part
  in a gesture or tap, or counts in the click finger count (edge contacts
  may still press the button). There are no strips, size
  limits or typing timers for palms on the host (no keyboard input reaches
  the engine at all; MultitouchSupport's MTDeviceDispatchKeyboardEvent is a
  stub returning kIOReturnUnsupported). With a kernel that passes those
  contacts on as MT_TOOL_PALM, libinput's tool-type palm detection does the
  same, so the strips of 0001 (side strips after typing, the bottom strip)
  now apply only to kernels that do not.

- On top, the host marks at most one finger "resting" per frame, the one
  nearest the front edge (MTPathStates_::checkForRestingThumb 0x7312c; the
  Resting bit 0x200 of its digitizer events is this flag, 0x704ac). For
  fingers other than the thumb, with exactly two fingers down:
    it rests while it lies within the strip along the front edge,
      zone = 12 mm * (H/76, or its square root below 1) + p + (p + 6) * k
      p = (min(|touchdown difference|, 0.2 s) / 0.2 s)^0.75
      k = clamp((major radius - 8 mm) / 10 mm, 0, 1)
    (12.13 to 20.1 mm here, isContactOnDivingButton 0x72c94), unless the
    other finger is beside it (under 19 mm away along y and 30 mm along x,
    countFingersOnDivingButton 0x72fb8), or it touched down higher than
    45 % of the pad's height (55 % with a palm below it), judged when the
    later of the two lands;
    it rests while the button is held down (from the frame after the
    press, so the click itself still counts both fingers);
    it stops resting while it moves along with the other finger: speed
    over 10 mm/s and sqrt(v u) / ((v + u)/2 + 1 mm/s) * cos(angle) above
    0.765, with u the faster other finger (computePathAndFingerSpeed-
    Symmetries 0x74574, breakOutOfRestingThumbBasedOnSymmetry 0x744e0).
  A resting finger moves nothing and counts for no gesture, tap or click,
  so a thumb resting on the click area while the index finger points
  points, clicks with one finger count, and drags while the button is held,
  instead of scrolling or right-clicking. Here it is a palm state,
  PALM_RESTING, recomputed every frame. ABS_MT_WIDTH_MAJOR is read for the
  radius: the kernel reports the radius in 0.01 mm doubled, as a diameter.

Not ported, as the kernel does not say which finger is the thumb: the
thumb's own rules (resting anywhere in the front 45 % while the other
fingers translate rather than pinch, MTRestZoneIntegrator_; a thumb lying
still for 0.25 s next to one finger becoming an edge contact until it
travels 8 mm), and the latch that keeps a finger resting once translation
dominates. Velocities come from libinput's history rather than the
firmware's.

